  "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1,$ we get\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}+6x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}+6x & =0 \\\\ 6x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-1,0,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,\\frac{1}{2}$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =2(-2)^{3}+3(-2)^{2}+1=-3 \\\\ f(-1) & =2(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+1=2 \\\\ f(0) & =2(0)^{3}+3(0)^{2}+1=1 \\\\ f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}+3{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=f(\\frac{1}{2})=2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-2)=-3,$ so the global minimum is $-3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14221929/assets/b7741f6f67f6070d6050ebaf792598593f98a406bdab91f73356e2c27085b7a0.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
